; The reference guards its map lookup with
;   len(str) >= 2 && len(str) <= 12 && str[0] >= 'a' && str[0] <= 'z'
; and every keyword satisfies it, so the guard is an optimization, not a
; rule — it is ported as one. Behind it: dispatch on length, then compare
; bytes. No String is constructed, so scanning an identifier allocates
; nothing at all.
;
; ★ The bounds below are DERIVED from the table (2..11); the reference writes
; the upper one as a literal 12. That is not a behavioural deviation — the
; guard's only requirement is that it exclude no keyword, and both bounds
; satisfy it, so the two agree on every input. Deriving it makes that
; property true by construction instead of true by coincidence: a pin bump
; that introduces a longer keyword regenerates a correct bound, where a
; transcribed literal would silently start rejecting it.

use scaly.containers.String

; True when the scanned bytes equal the ASCII literal `word`.
;
; ★★★BOTH SIDES ARE A VIEW since 2026-09-05. `buf` was a bare
; `pointer[char]` whose length lived in the CALLER -- the shape
; tools/lenfix/scan.py reports as a chain: this body proved `buf` a
; buffer while its own signature carried no length, and
; get_identifier_token one level up carried the length while proving
; nothing. Neither could convert alone.
;
; ★The length test is not redundant even though every caller
; dispatches on length first: it is what lets the subscript's bounds
; check fold away, since the loop then runs against the SAME length the
; check reads. Without it the two lengths are unrelated to the optimizer.
function kw_eq(buf: Slice[char], word: Slice[char]) returns bool linked













